LMN 1
title: Operation Paper Lantern
artist: Shiori examples
tempo: 144
time: 4/4
key: C major (trio in F major; the grandioso lifts to D major)
mood: a caper on Lantern Night; fast, cheeky, more and more out of control, then a grand finish

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FESTIVAL HEIST. A comic brass march for a blind countess in oven mitts,
// built like a Sousa march: first strain, second strain, trio in the
// subdominant, a "dogfight" break strain, then the grandioso. Taiko and
// shime-daiko (percussion lanes), shamisen (banjo), festival flute, piccolo,
// woodblock and a brass band.
//
// SUZU'S FIGURE (LEITMOTIFS.md 1) is the march tune on trumpets. In C it is a
// step down from the canonical pitches, with the same rhythm:
//     C5q D5q E5q G5q | A5h. D5q~ | D5h ...     (do re mi sol | la . re~)
// The falling note is never allowed to resolve. Each time it hangs over the
// dominant (once over V of V) and the gang answers instead: a woodblock "tok, tok-tok" and a piccolo scurry.
// It turns up four ways:
//   A1/A2      the march tune, four quarters and the long top note
//   Sneak      in A minor on tiptoe (A B C E | F | B), staccato trombone and
//              pizzicato, then flute; it ends in a "wah-wah-wah-waaah"
//   Dogfight   only the climb, in eighths, sequenced down a D C Bb A bass and
//              then in sixteenths: the staircase that will not stop
//   Grandioso  the march transposed up a step (transpose=2), so the figure
//              sounds at its true pitch, D5 E5 F#5 A5 | B5 | E5, for the
//              Countess's triumph. In the Tag she climbs once more, holds the
//              B over A9, and this time leaps up to D6 instead of falling.
//              (The E still never steps down to D: that is saved for suzu_song.)
//
// HINATA'S TUNE (HINATA.md) on accordion in the Trio, in F major at its own
// pitch, using the 4/4 "half" version (triplet eighths keep the 6/8 lilt). The
// shamisen picks her kitchen chord F3 A3 C4 G4 the way her guitar does. Her
// tired bar (F minor add9, the tune frozen on G5) comes in each pass, played
// by the accordion itself at pp, with the claps stopping. In the second pass
// the flute adds her second voice a third below, and the last A5 is
// hijacked: the chord under it turns to A7 and the dogfight starts in D minor.
//
// FORM  79 bars, about 2:10. Tempo 144, then 146, 144, 148 accelerating to 164
//       in the dogfight, 152 for the grandioso, broadening to 116 at the end.
//   Intro       4  taiko call, shamisen riff, a flute slide; bugle call
//   A1         16  the march: figure x2 with answers; the boast; the figure
//                  again too fast, a scramble to the top and a stop
//   Sneak       8  the plan, on tiptoe; sad trombone
//   A2          8  the march again, bigger: flute an octave up, piccolo
//                  trills, trombone smears where the woodblock was
//   Trio       16  Hinata's tune on accordion, twice
//   Dogfight    8  the chase, accelerating, everything climbing at once
//   Grandioso  16  A1 a step higher (D major) with full band, piccolo
//                  obbligato, trombone counter-melody, glockenspiel glints
//   Tag         3  the last climb, the held B, and the high D
// The script cuts the cue with a 0.2 s fade at the handshake, so nothing
// waits for a cadence; every section hands straight on to the next. The final
// hit leaves three slow beats for the tails, and the loop restarts on taiko
// alone, so the jump from D back to C is never heard as a wrong chord.
//
// HARMONY
//   A1:   C | F | G7 | C | Am | F | D7 | G7 | F | C/E | Dm7 | G7 | C F | G7 | G7 | C
//   Sneak: Am | F | E7 | Am | Am | F | E7 | E7
//   A2:   C | F | G7 | C | Am | F | G7 | C7
//   Trio: Fadd9 | Gm7 C7 | Bb(add9) Am7 | Gm7 C7 Fm(add9) |
//         Fadd9 | Gm7 C7 | Bb(add9) Am7 | Gm7 C7 Fadd9      (2nd pass: ... A7)
//   Dogfight: Dm Dm7 | C C7 | Bb | A A7 | Dm C | Bb A | A7 | A7
//   Grandioso: A1 + 2 semitones.   Tag: G | A9 | D
//
// JOBS  tpt: the tune. tpt2: thirds and sixths under it. horns: the "pah" on
// beats 2 and 4. tuba: the "oom". sham: running eighths (triplets in the trio).
// picc: the gang's answers, trills and obbligato. fue: festival flute colour
// and doubling. tbn: sneak tune, smears, counter-melodies. acc: Hinata, then
// swept into the band's afterbeats for the chase and the triumph.
// pizz: tiptoe. glock: lantern glints. taiko: kick = o-daiko, tom_low = taiko
// "don", tom_mid = "doko" fills, tom_hi = shime-daiko, rim = "ka", woodblock,
// clap. kit: march snare and cymbals.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

track tpt    trumpet       vol=84 pan=-6  reverb=22
track tpt2   trumpet       vol=60 pan=12  reverb=22
track horns  brass         vol=50 pan=20  reverb=24
track tbn    trombone      vol=64 pan=26  reverb=22
track tuba   tuba          vol=76 pan=2   reverb=12
track sham   banjo         vol=56 pan=-32 reverb=16
track fue    flute         vol=62 pan=-18 reverb=30
track picc   piccolo       vol=52 pan=30  reverb=26
track acc    accordion     vol=68 pan=-12 reverb=24
track pizz   pizzicato     vol=64 pan=-24 reverb=18
track glock  glockenspiel  vol=42 pan=34  reverb=40
track taiko  percussion    vol=82 pan=0   reverb=30
track kit    drums         vol=58 pan=6   reverb=18

